WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:07.769
<v Maya Okafor>Alright, happy Friday everyone! Let’s get started. Quick round of updates before we dive in?</v>

00:00:08.222 --> 00:00:20.145
<v Lukas Brenner>Sure, I’ll go first. Not much new on my end, just wrapping up the EMEA open enrollment comms. Still getting a few questions about the new dental plan, but nothing major.</v>

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:32.020
<v Priya Raman>Same here, pretty quiet week. I did get a ping from the Singapore team about the wellness stipend, but I pointed them to the FAQ. Otherwise, all good.</v>

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:43.631
<v Daniel Ferreira>I’m still working on the comp benchmarking refresh. Should have the first draft ready by next week. Oh, and I finally got the Mercer data, so that’ll help.</v>

00:00:44.149 --> 00:00:52.226
<v Tomás Rivera>Legal’s all clear on my side. I did review the new NDA template, but nothing urgent for Total Rewards right now.</v>

00:00:53.278 --> 00:01:01.740
<v Maya Okafor>Great, thanks everyone. I’ll just add that I met with Finance about the Q4 budget, and we’re in good shape for now.</v>

00:01:01.947 --> 00:01:11.178
<v Maya Okafor>Okay, let’s move to the main agenda item: the Ireland pension policy update. Lukas, can you give us a quick recap of what’s changing?</v>

00:01:12.354 --> 00:01:23.508
<v Lukas Brenner>Yep. So, starting January, Ireland’s rolling out the My Future Fund auto enrolment. Basically, if someone’s not in our Aviva plan, they’ll get put into the government scheme automatically.</v>

00:01:24.709 --> 00:01:35.863
<v Lukas Brenner>Our plan’s still better—higher employer match, more flexibility, tax relief, all that. But we need to update the handbook to reflect the new regulation and clarify the match range.</v>

00:01:36.202 --> 00:01:45.817
<v Priya Raman>Is the main change just the employer match wording? I saw it’s going from a flat 5% to a 3-5% range in the new text.</v>

00:01:46.570 --> 00:01:52.339
<v Daniel Ferreira>Yeah, that’s what I noticed too. Is that because of the new hire default, or…?</v>

00:01:52.768 --> 00:02:03.152
<v Lukas Brenner>Exactly. New hires start at 3%, but they can increase up to 5%. We wanted to make it clearer, since some folks thought it was always 5%.</v>

00:02:04.597 --> 00:02:14.213
<v Tomás Rivera>From a legal standpoint, as long as we’re transparent about the range and the opt-up option, we’re fine. Just make sure the language isn’t misleading.</v>

00:02:15.086 --> 00:02:21.240
<v Maya Okafor>Good call, Tomás. Lukas, can you double-check that the handbook page spells out the opt-up process?</v>

00:02:21.505 --> 00:02:28.044
<v Lukas Brenner>Will do. I’ll add a line about how to increase contributions and where to find the form.</v>

00:02:28.568 --> 00:02:35.875
<v Priya Raman>One thing—do we need to mention the government scheme’s opt-out window? I remember there’s a 6-month lock-in or something.</v>

00:02:37.178 --> 00:02:47.563
<v Tomás Rivera>Yeah, but only if someone gets auto-enrolled in My Future Fund. If they’re in our plan, it doesn’t apply. Maybe just a note in the FAQ link?</v>

00:02:48.356 --> 00:02:56.049
<v Lukas Brenner>I can add a sentence to the FAQ doc, but keep the handbook page focused on our plan. That work?</v>

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:04.598
<v Maya Okafor>That makes sense. We don’t want to overload the main page. Just make sure the FAQ is linked clearly.</v>

00:03:05.666 --> 00:03:13.358
<v Daniel Ferreira>And the employer match—should we say 'up to 5%' or '3-5%'? I think '3-5%' is more accurate for new hires.</v>

00:03:14.843 --> 00:03:19.843
<v Lukas Brenner>Yeah, I’ll use '3-5% employer matching contribution' so it’s clear there’s a range.</v>

00:03:20.817 --> 00:03:22.355
<v Priya Raman>Sounds good to me.</v>

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.329
<v Tomás Rivera>No objections from Legal.</v>

00:03:26.687 --> 00:03:37.841
<v Maya Okafor>Alright, so to recap: we’re updating the handbook to reflect the 3-5% match, clarify the opt-up process, and link to the FAQ for the government scheme details. Everyone aligned?</v>

00:03:38.838 --> 00:03:40.338
<v Daniel Ferreira>Yep, all good.</v>

00:03:41.473 --> 00:03:42.973
<v Priya Raman>Agreed.</v>

00:03:43.829 --> 00:03:45.329
<v Tomás Rivera>Agreed.</v>

00:03:46.609 --> 00:03:53.916
<v Lukas Brenner>I’m on it. I’ll get the draft out by end of next week and ping you all for review.</v>

00:03:54.829 --> 00:03:57.521
<v Maya Okafor>Perfect. Thanks, Lukas. That’s our decision then.</v>

00:03:58.887 --> 00:04:06.580
<v Maya Okafor>Okay, last agenda item—quick one. We got the results from the annual benefits survey. Participation was up, which is great.</v>

00:04:07.746 --> 00:04:09.670
<v Priya Raman>Oh, nice! Any big surprises?</v>

00:04:10.487 --> 00:04:18.948
<v Maya Okafor>Not really. Most folks are happy with the core benefits, but there were a few comments about wanting more mental health support.</v>

00:04:19.485 --> 00:04:26.408
<v Daniel Ferreira>That’s come up before. Maybe we can look at adding more EAP sessions or something for next year.</v>

00:04:26.930 --> 00:04:34.622
<v Lukas Brenner>I can pull together a summary of the mental health feedback and some vendor options. Give me, uh, two weeks?</v>

00:04:35.651 --> 00:04:40.651
<v Maya Okafor>That’d be great, Lukas. Let’s put it on the agenda for the 9th.</v>

00:04:41.846 --> 00:04:49.539
<v Tomás Rivera>Just flagging, if we do add new vendors, Legal will need to review the contracts. But that’s down the line.</v>

00:04:50.416 --> 00:04:52.339
<v Priya Raman>Thanks, Tomás. Good to know.</v>

00:04:53.354 --> 00:04:57.200
<v Maya Okafor>Alright, I think that’s everything. Anything else before we wrap?</v>

00:04:57.757 --> 00:04:59.257
<v Daniel Ferreira>Nothing from me.</v>

00:04:59.558 --> 00:05:01.058
<v Priya Raman>All good here.</v>

00:05:01.630 --> 00:05:03.130
<v Lukas Brenner>Nope, I’m set.</v>

00:05:03.683 --> 00:05:05.183
<v Tomás Rivera>I’m good.</v>

00:05:05.798 --> 00:05:08.491
<v Maya Okafor>Okay, thanks everyone. Have a great weekend!</v>

00:05:09.393 --> 00:05:10.893
<v Priya Raman>You too!</v>

00:05:11.273 --> 00:05:12.773
<v Daniel Ferreira>Bye all.</v>

00:05:13.273 --> 00:05:14.812
<v Lukas Brenner>See you next week.</v>

00:05:15.914 --> 00:05:17.414
<v Tomás Rivera>Take care.</v>
